hot-1 — Profile & attribute the ASDF load/compile (plan-3; DO FIRST, gates all 3 plans).

Measure before optimizing.  Establishes the baseline every other issue reports against.

WHAT
- Instrument the load path with cheap phase timers (a small ring of named counters,
  printed on demand): time spent in READ (the reader), MACROEXPAND-ALL, EVAL of
  top-level forms, and CLOS setup (defclass/defmethod/make-instance/dispatch).  Also
  count: forms read, symbols interned, conses allocated, funcalls, GC time.
- Drive (require :asdf) (or the closest reproducible load) three ways where possible
  — source, text fasl, and later binary fasl — and dump the attribution.
- Add an eval-hot-path micro-profiler: sample or count time in variable lookup
  (environment_find_variable), argument-list construction, funcall dispatch, and the
  DIVOP/arith fast paths — to confirm the ~150us/funcall breakdown on THIS workload.
- Optionally a build flag BOCL_PROFILE that compiles in the counters (off by default,
  zero cost when off).

DELIVERABLE: a written baseline table (phase -> seconds/%, on a named machine) in the
issue, so plan-1/2/3 each cite "before/after" against it.  This tells us whether the
lever is the reader, the macroexpander, the evaluator, or CLOS — and thus how much of
each plan matters.

ACCEPTANCE: reproducible attribution of the ASDF load and compile times; numbers
recorded; zero overhead when BOCL_PROFILE is off; suite green.

DEPENDS ON: none (first thing to do across the whole performance program).

== IMPLEMENTED (v1.6.0) ==
Phase-exclusive wall-clock profiler + event counters, gated by -DBOCL_PROFILE
(zero cost by default — the PROFILE_* macros expand to nothing).

- New sources/profile.{h,c}: a phase STACK charges exclusive time to the phase on
  top (nested READ/GC pause the enclosing EVAL); CLOCK_MONOTONIC (POSIX).  Phases:
  other/read/macroexpand/eval/gc/clos.  Counters: allocations/interns/funcalls/
  gc-runs/forms.  Built into common_SRCS.
- C instrumentation (all no-op unless -DBOCL_PROFILE, so default builds are
  unchanged): READ + EVAL bracket each top-level form in bocl_load_cstring's load
  loop; garbage_collect / minor_collect bracket GC; allocate() bumps allocations;
  symbol_intern bumps interns; the three appliers (kernel_apply_function/closure/
  built_in) bump funcalls; per-form counter.
- Lisp interface (BOCL package, exported): BOCL:PROFILE-RESET, BOCL:PROFILE-REPORT
  [&optional stream], BOCL:PROFILE-ENABLED-P, and the WITH-PROFILE-PHASE macro to
  bracket a coarser phase (e.g. :MACROEXPAND, :CLOS) from Lisp.  Kernel primitives
  %PROFILE-RESET / -REPORT / -ENABLED-P / -PHASE-ENTER / -PHASE-EXIT.
- Enable with: make build CPPFLAGS=-DBOCL_PROFILE.  Both builds are warning-clean.

== BASELINE (prelude load ~9s, Linux x86-64; the ASDF baseline should be re-run on
   the M3 with the same tooling, source / text-fasl / binary-fasl) ==
  phase          time      %
  read          0.147 s   1.6
  eval          8.818 s  96.3     <-- dominant
  gc            0.190 s   2.1
  clos          0.000 s   0.0     (not bracketed on a bare load)
  TOTAL         9.158 s
  allocations 3,826,083   interns 1,922   funcalls 787,389   gc-runs 14   forms 1,313

FINDING: source load is EVAL-BOUND (~96%) — the tree-walker (with lazy macro
displacement folded into EVAL) dominates; READ and GC are minor here.  Implications
for the program:
- plan-2 (byte-code VM) and plan-3 (hot-path eval opts) attack the 96% — they are
  the real levers for run/compile speed.
- plan-1 (compact fasl) attacks READ + re-intern + re-macroexpand: only ~1.6% on
  this SOURCE load, but that share is much larger for the BLOATED ASDF *text* fasl
  (the reason the text fasl was slower than source) — so plan-1 still fixes the
  fasl-slower-than-source regression, while the VM is what moves the eval needle.
Re-run this attribution on the actual ASDF load (M3) to confirm the split there
(expect read/intern larger for the text fasl; eval still dominant).
